## Conclusions
The face-directed self-assembly of this novel inorganic polyhedral nanocube-based framework, built from well-defined molecular inorganic parts, demonstrates that cyclic SBUs with intrinsic holes can be used for the design of functional inorganic framework materials. The physical properties of the highly anionic and porous SBU, $\mathrm{\{P_{8}\mathrm{\hat{W_{48}}O_{184}\}^{\bar{4}0-\bar{\ell}}}}$ , and the electrochemical aspects of the redox-switchable $\mathrm{{Mn}(\mathrm{{II})}}$ linkers both impart important characteristics on 1. The net effect is a robust electrochemically switchable open framework material with the ability to controllably take up small molecules. We are at present working towards establishing the general design process for all-inorganic framework materials based on related molecular metal oxide SBUs and further studies aim to investigate the catalytic, sensing and guest exchange capabilities of 1 in more detail.
## Methods
All chemicals and solvents were of analytical grade (Sigma Aldrich) and were used as supplied, without further purification. $\mathrm{K}_{28}\mathrm{Li}_{5}[\mathrm{H}_{7}\mathrm{P}_{8}\mathrm{W}_{48}\mathrm{O}_{184}].92\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was synthesized from a modified method adapted from the original $\mathrm{\{\bar{P}_{8}W_{48}O_{184}\}^{40-}}$ paper published by Contant and $\mathrm{Teze}^{28}$ .
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction. Suitable single crystals of 1 were selected and mounted onto the end of a thin glass fibre using Fomblin oil. X-ray diffraction intensity data for compound 1 were collected using an Oxford Diffraction Gemini Ultra with an ATLAS charge-coupled device (CCD) detector $[\lambda(\mathrm{MoK}\alpha)=0.71073\mathring\mathrm{A}]$ at 150(2) K. Data reduction was performed using the CrysAlis software package and structure solution, and refinement was carried out using SHELXS-97 (ref. 31) and SHELXL-97 (ref. 32) using $\mathrm{WinGX}^{33}$ . Corrections for incident and diffracted beam absorption effects were applied using analytical numeric absorption correction using a multifaceted crystal model34.
Powder X-ray diffraction. PXRD patterns were collected on a Bruker AXS D8 powder diffractometer $(\lambda(\mathrm{CuK}\alpha)=1.5405\mathrm{~\AA~}$ ) equipped with a graphite monochromator in capillary mode at room temperature.
Flame atomic absorption spectroscopy analysis. FAAS analysis was performed at the Environmental Chemistry Section, Department of Chemistry, University of Glasgow, on a Perkin-Elmer 1100B atomic absorption spectrophotometer.
Flame photometry. A Corning Flame Photometer 410 at the Environmental Chemistry Section, Department of Chemistry, University of Glasgow, was used to determine the potassium and lithium content of the materials.
Thermogravimetric analysis. TGA was performed on a TA Q500 instrument under a nitrogen atmosphere. The initial heating range was from room temperature to $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at $2.00^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ per min, followed by a second range from $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to $1,000^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at $5.00^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ per min.
Differential scanning calorimetry analysis. DSC analysis was performed on a TA Instruments Q 200 calorimeter under nitrogen flow at a heating rate of $20\mathrm{^{\circ}C\operatorname*{min}^{-1}}$
Fourier-transform infrared spectroscopy. The materials were prepared as KBr pellets and FT-IR spectra were collected in transmission mode using a JASCO FT-IR-410 spectrometer. Wavenumbers (n) are given in $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and intensities are denoted as $\mathbf{w}=$ weak, $\mathrm{m}=$ medium, $s=$ strong, br $\c=$ broad, $s\mathrm{h}=$ sharp.
UV-Vis spectroscopy. Solution phase UV-Vis spectra and photometric studies were collected using a Shimadzu UV-3101PC spectrophotometer in transmission mode using quartz cuvettes with an optical path length of $1.0\mathrm{cm}$ . Solid-state UV-Vis spectra were collected on a Jasco V-670 spectrophotometer.
${}^{\mathbf{1}}\mathbf{H}$ -nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy ( ${\bf\Pi^{1}H}$ -NMR). $^1\mathrm{H}$ -NMR spectroscopy was performed on a Bruker DPX 400 spectrometer using the solvent signal as internal standard. All d values are given in ppm.
Synthesis of $\mathrm{K_{18}L i_{6}[M n_{8}(H_{2}O)_{48}P_{8}W_{48}O_{184}]{\cdot}108H_{2}O}$ (1). A $2\mathrm{M}$ aqueous LiAc solution $(15~\mathrm{ml})$ ) was adjusted to $\mathrm{pH}4.0$ using $100\%$ acetic acid. To this solution was then added $\mathrm{Mn}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $102\mathrm{mg},0.4\mathrm{mmol})$ ) followed by $\mathrm{K_{28}L i_{5}[H_{7}P_{8}W_{48}O_{184}]}.92\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $\mathrm{100~mg}$ , $7\:\upmu\mathrm{mol}\dot{}.$ ). The very pale yellow mixture was then heated at $90~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight $(\sim20\mathrm{h})$ . The mixture was slowly cooled to room temperature, transferred to a glass vial and then into a refrigerator $({\sim}4^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ ; crystals began to form after one day. These well-formed pale-yellow cubic crystals were separated three days after crystallization began (yield, 52 mg, $3.28~\upmu\mathrm{mol}$ $46.4\%$ based on tungsten)). Diffraction quality crystals were grown by the slow diffusion of methanol into the reaction mother liquor and by slow evaporation of the mother liquor over a two- to three-week period.
Characterization. Characteristic IR bands $(\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ : 2,010(s), 1,628(s), 1,419(m), $^{1,350(\mathrm{w})}$ , 1,135(s), 1,080(s), 1,021(s), 953(s), 930(s). UV bands $\mathbf{\lambda}\lambda\left(\mathbf{nm}\right)$ , 1 $:(\mathrm{M}^{-1}\thinspace\mathrm{cm}^{-1})$ ): 560 (2.50), 350 (4.00). Elemental analysis for the partially dehydrated material, $\mathrm{K_{18}L i_{6}[M n_{8}(H_{2}O)_{48}P_{8}W_{48}O_{184}]}.90H_{2}O$ $M_{\mathrm{W}}{=}15{,}688{.}91~\mathrm{gmol^{-1}}$ ; calcd. (found, partial solvent water lost): manganese 2.80 (2.80), tungsten 56.28 (54.50), potassium 4.47 (4.52), lithium 0.28 $(0.28)\%$ . TGA water loss from 0 to $250^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\colon$ calcd. (found), 12.14 $(12.32\%)$ . Redox titrations: the cerimetric titration of a solution of compound 1 showed the presence of eight electrons, which (formally) corresponds to $8\:\mathrm{Mn(II)}$ centres.
